  <content>
  <section><title>Identifying the Constant Ratio of the Exponential Function</title>
<para>What is the constant ratio (also called the growth factor) of the exponential function shown in the table? Rewrite each term to show the initial value and repeated use of the constant ratio.</para>
<table class="os-raise-horizontaltable">
  <tgroup cols="6"><tbody><row>
      <entry><emphasis>\(x\)</emphasis> </entry><entry> 0 </entry>
      <entry> 1 </entry>
      <entry> 2 </entry>
      <entry> 3 </entry>
      <entry> 4 </entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry><emphasis>\(f(x)\)</emphasis> </entry><entry> 4 </entry>
      <entry> 12 </entry>
      <entry> 36 </entry>
      <entry> 108 </entry>
      <entry> 324 </entry>
    </row>
  </tbody></tgroup></table>

<title>Defining Exponential Growth</title>
<para>Because the output of exponential functions increases very rapidly, the term “exponential growth” is often used in everyday language to describe anything that grows or increases rapidly. However, exponential growth can be defined more precisely in a mathematical sense. If the growth rate is proportional to the amount present, the function models exponential growth.</para>



